<!-- THE BIOELECTRIC OS -->
<section id="thesis">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <div class="section-label">First Principles</div>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="two-col-text fade-in">
        <h2>The Bioelectric<br><em>Operating System</em></h2>
        <p>Levin's work reveals the hidden layer: bioelectricity is not housekeeping. Voltage gradients across cell membranes and gap junctions form a distributed goal-state language sitting above the genome. Change the pattern and you change collective intent — grow an eye here, regenerate a limb there, solve that task.</p>
        <p>Spikes are fast local I/O. Gradients are the slower, analogue OS encoding large-scale purpose. Neuralink's clinical data validated this in real time: when single-neuron spikes became unreadable, switching to aggregate spike-band power <em>improved</em> cursor control. The collective murmur carried more reliable intention than any isolated voice.</p>
        <p>If these gradient patterns can be measured and written reproducibly, they become a programmable control surface for morphogenesis, repair, and computation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="two-col-aside fade-in">
        <div class="pull-quote">
          "Evolution did not invent a central clock or a separate software layer. It built intelligence into the physics of the substrate itself."
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;margin-top:8px;">
          <div style="padding:20px 24px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:8px;background:var(--surface);">
            <div style="font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.15em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:8px;">Silicon Limit</div>
            <div style="font-size:14px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;">Von Neumann tax: constant data movement between memory and logic. Scaling costs grow super-linearly. Frontier training strains power grids.</div>
          </div>
          <div style="padding:20px 24px;border:1px solid rgba(79,209,197,0.2);border-radius:8px;background:rgba(79,209,197,0.03);">
            <div style="font-size:11px;letter-spacing:0.15em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--accent2);margin-bottom:8px;">Biology's Answer</div>
            <div style="font-size:14px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;">Memory and compute co-located in the same physical medium. Self-repair while operating. ~20W for continual learning under drift.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider"></div>

